course: dictionary
lesson: 4970
title: Inclusion: everyone belongs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Inclusion is a word for making sure nobody is left out. Read the sentence: «Inclusion means making sure people aren't left out; empathy means understanding how someone else feels.» The clue is in the words "aren't left out", so inclusion means the opposite of being left out.
explain: Inclusion can mean being included, or making someone part of a group. It also means making sure everyone, including people with disabilities or from different backgrounds, can take part and feel welcome.
explain: Inclusion is something people do, not just a word. A school that practises inclusion checks that every child can join in lessons, games and clubs. To test your meaning, swap it into the sentence: "Making sure people aren't left out means making sure people aren't left out" still makes sense, so you are on the right track.
fact: Inclusion means being included, or making someone part of a group.
fact: Inclusion can mean making sure everyone, including people with disabilities or from different backgrounds, can take part and feel welcome.
fact: Inclusion can also mean something that is included or added.
fact: Useful phrases: social inclusion, a policy of inclusion, inclusion of.
fact: «Empathy» means understanding how someone else feels, and it helps people practise inclusion.
exercise: «Inclusion means making sure people aren't left out.» What does inclusion mean? | Choosing the fastest players for a team | *Making sure everyone is part of the group | Making a list of rules | Being left out of the group
exercise: A school has a policy of inclusion. What does this suggest? | Lessons are cancelled on Fridays | Only the strongest children can take part | *Everyone, including children with disabilities, can take part and feel welcome
exercise: Find and copy the word that means understanding how someone else feels. | feels | *empathy | inclusion | people
test: In maths, inclusion can mean something is part of a set or list. What does this mean? | *Something is part of a set or list | Something has no shape | Something is the biggest number in the set | Something is left out of the set
test: Which is the best example of social inclusion? | Only one team gets to play at break | *A new pupil is asked to join the game at break and feels welcome | A new pupil is left to sit alone at lunch | Pupils are told to keep their ideas to themselves
exam: Why is inclusion important in a school? | Because it stops children from making friends | *Because every child belongs and can join in | Because it means lessons are shorter | Because only the top pupils need help
